  "query_preview": "(Today through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 301 AM EDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nToday through Friday night... \n\nStrong surface high pressure building in favors quiet weather \nconditions during the period. Current surface analysis early this \nmorning depicts a cold front pushing into central Indiana from the \nnorth filtering in cooler-drier air. Dewpoints across northern \nportions of the state have already fallen well into the 50s. Look \nfor dewpoints within the forecast area to also fall well into the \n50s over the next few hours. Diurnal mixing later today may even \ndrop dewpoints into the upper 40s. This much drier airmass along \nwith highs generally remaining in the 70s due to cold air advection \nwill feel very comfortable compared to the past few days. \n\nExpect an upper wave approaching from the west on Friday to result \nin increasing mid-high clouds. Most guidance suggest subsidence \ninduced dry air from the surface high should inhibit any \nprecipitation chances from the weakening disturbance so the forecast \nremains dry. Look for temperatures to remain cooler than normal with \nhighs generally in the mid 70s to low 80s through the end of the \nwork week.  \n\nSaturday through early next week...\n\nLong range guidance depicts weak surface high pressure remaining the \ndominant weather feature for much of the weekend providing mostly \nquiet weather conditions. Aloft, an amplified ridge centered near \nthe Rockies with northwesterly flow across the Great Lakes Region \nshould allow for a few shortwaves to pass through the region. These \ndisturbances along with deeper moisture advecting into central \nIndiana will return low rain chances to the forecast at times late \nSunday into early next week. \n\nDiverging model solutions leads to lower confidence in exact details \ntowards the end of the period. Widespread severe weather is not \nexpected at this time, but ensemble guidance suggest that at least \nmoderate destabilization will occur each afternoon Sunday into early \nnext week. This along with enhancement of mid-upper level flow from \nthe passing shortwaves could support a threat for severe weather. \nUncertainty will remain high until models become better aligned. \n\nWarm air advection ahead of the aforementioned disturbances should \nwarm temperatures well into the 80s to lower 90s in some spots \nSunday and Monday. Dewpoints returning to the low-mid 70s may also \nlead to peak heat indices around 100 degrees.",
